WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:01.920
- House, I'm looking at you, sir.

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:03.970
Are you going to give
me a zebra or a horse?

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:05.160
I don't know, let's check it out

00:00:07.642 --> 00:00:09.169
Woo!

00:00:09.169 --> 00:00:10.269
- [Paramedic] What, are you kidding me?

00:00:10.269 --> 00:00:12.049
Tub-o-goo gas gotta be over six bills.

00:00:12.049 --> 00:00:13.599
You ain't gonna lift him
with a couple of blankets.

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:14.443
- Now this is tough.

00:00:14.443 --> 00:00:15.679
- [Paramedic] You got
a better idea Einstein?

00:00:15.679 --> 00:00:16.771
Yeah, just roll him off.

00:00:16.771 --> 00:00:18.089
(laughter)

00:00:18.089 --> 00:00:18.922
What? He's already dead,

00:00:18.922 --> 00:00:19.755
ain't like he's going to feel it.

00:00:19.755 --> 00:00:20.979
- It's actually a situation that

00:00:20.979 --> 00:00:23.139
some of my paramedic
friends discussed with me,

00:00:23.139 --> 00:00:24.959
about legitimately having trouble

00:00:24.959 --> 00:00:27.499
getting humans outside of their homes,

00:00:27.499 --> 00:00:29.479
into the ambulance and into the ER.

00:00:29.479 --> 00:00:30.789
There's even been times that

00:00:30.789 --> 00:00:33.509
I've heard them talk
about injuring themselves,

00:00:33.509 --> 00:00:35.829
bringing patients out of their homes.

00:00:35.829 --> 00:00:38.209
Because they were perhaps
overweight or obese,

00:00:38.209 --> 00:00:41.211
or perhaps their environment wasn't safe.

00:00:41.211 --> 00:00:42.893
- Alright, one, two, lift.

00:00:42.893 --> 00:00:44.311
(fart noise)

00:00:44.311 --> 00:00:45.869
(laughter)

00:00:45.869 --> 00:00:46.959
- Come on man.

00:00:46.959 --> 00:00:48.279
- Me? You're the kielbasa king.

00:00:48.279 --> 00:00:49.112
- It wasn't me.

00:00:50.519 --> 00:00:51.919
- Well, it wasn't me either.

00:00:55.839 --> 00:00:56.789
- Don't look at me.

00:00:58.179 --> 00:01:01.329
- Is the patient alive? Did
no one check for a pulse?

00:01:01.329 --> 00:01:03.939
- Trust me, skin's cold,
pupils are fixed and dilated.

00:01:03.939 --> 00:01:04.809
He's not breathing.

00:01:04.809 --> 00:01:05.642
- He's got a pulse.

00:01:05.642 --> 00:01:08.009
- He's not dead if he has a pulse.

00:01:08.009 --> 00:01:10.629
- 46 year old guy in a coma.

00:01:10.629 --> 00:01:12.499
Doesn't appear to be
anything wrong with him,

00:01:12.499 --> 00:01:14.999
except the fact that he
weighs over 600 pounds.

00:01:14.999 --> 00:01:15.849
- Who says that?

00:01:15.849 --> 00:01:18.389
Who, who talks about a
patient's weight like that

00:01:18.389 --> 00:01:20.239
and says there's probably nothing wrong?

00:01:20.239 --> 00:01:22.029
Do you know that? Do you know his history?

00:01:22.029 --> 00:01:23.869
He can't even give you his full history.

00:01:23.869 --> 00:01:26.409
There is no way you could
have results this quickly.

00:01:26.409 --> 00:01:28.729
Like, at least get an echocardiogram.

00:01:28.729 --> 00:01:31.189
- Blood sugar's normal,
cholesterol is lower than mine,

00:01:31.189 --> 00:01:33.389
tox screens clean, no sign of trauma.

00:01:33.389 --> 00:01:34.999
- [Foreman] Sure there
wasn't a mix-up in the lab?

00:01:34.999 --> 00:01:35.969
- Three times?

00:01:35.969 --> 00:01:37.079
- I'm glad that they rechecked

00:01:37.079 --> 00:01:38.849
because if a clinical picture,

00:01:38.849 --> 00:01:40.719
meaning what a patient
looks like, behaves like,

00:01:40.719 --> 00:01:43.249
or symptoms are like,
don't match the labs,

00:01:43.249 --> 00:01:45.059
you should be rechecking those labs.

00:01:45.059 --> 00:01:47.019
Too many times I've had that happen.

00:01:47.019 --> 00:01:49.419
Recheck the labs and find out
that they're completely normal

00:01:49.419 --> 00:01:51.179
or the other way completely abnormal.

00:01:51.179 --> 00:01:53.229
So I always like to keep laboratory error

00:01:53.229 --> 00:01:54.499
in the back of my mind.

00:01:54.499 --> 00:01:57.059
Or in the front of my
mind, forefront of my mind.

00:01:57.059 --> 00:01:57.892
My mind.

00:01:57.892 --> 00:02:00.589
- Nothing abnormal in the
EEG or the neurological exam.

00:02:00.589 --> 00:02:03.629
- Who gets an EEG when
a patient just rolls in?

00:02:03.629 --> 00:02:04.462
It's too soon.

00:02:04.462 --> 00:02:07.249
You should get a CAT scan,
see what the lungs are like.

00:02:07.249 --> 00:02:10.309
Get an echo, see with the
anatomy of the heart is like.

00:02:10.309 --> 00:02:12.069
You should see what the digestive system

00:02:12.069 --> 00:02:13.509
is like with a CAT scan.

00:02:13.509 --> 00:02:16.419
When a patient comes in and
you don't know what's going on,

00:02:16.419 --> 00:02:19.469
you have to throw all
the tests at the patient

00:02:19.469 --> 00:02:20.949
in order to understand what's going on.

00:02:20.949 --> 00:02:22.619
You need to check for substance abuse.

00:02:22.619 --> 00:02:25.039
You need to check for toxic metabolites,

00:02:25.039 --> 00:02:26.391
Complete metabolic panel,
the complete blood count.

00:02:26.391 --> 00:02:30.039
Look at all these values and
not just jump to conclusions.

00:02:30.039 --> 00:02:33.139
I mean like doing EEG
seems like the 50th step

00:02:33.139 --> 00:02:35.049
and they've skipped over one through 49.

00:02:35.049 --> 00:02:37.189
- Improperly prepared
puffer fish can have toxins

00:02:37.189 --> 00:02:38.149
that could cause a coma

00:02:38.149 --> 00:02:39.679
and might not show up on the tox screen.

00:02:39.679 --> 00:02:40.939
- And if it was a puffer fish,

00:02:40.939 --> 00:02:42.399
he'd be dead in six to eight hours tops.

00:02:42.399 --> 00:02:44.019
He's been in a coma at least 24.

00:02:44.019 --> 00:02:47.249
- How do people know these
facts about puffer fish?

00:02:47.249 --> 00:02:49.499
Come on Foreman, you didn't know that.

00:02:49.499 --> 00:02:51.669
You Googled it. He Googled it.

00:02:51.669 --> 00:02:53.639
- It's ridiculous, a
person shouldn't be able

00:02:53.639 --> 00:02:55.249
to eat themselves into oblivion

00:02:55.249 --> 00:02:56.419
and then just expect everyone

00:02:56.419 --> 00:02:58.169
to pull out the stops to fix everything.

00:02:58.169 --> 00:02:59.269
- What we were supposed to do?

00:02:59.269 --> 00:03:01.099
Refuse treatment to anyone who's obese?

00:03:01.099 --> 00:03:03.939
- [Chase] Come on, give me a
break, the guy's not obese.

00:03:03.939 --> 00:03:05.599
He's not even morbidly obese.

00:03:05.599 --> 00:03:06.679
He's suicidal.

00:03:06.679 --> 00:03:08.599
- People who attempt suicide get treated.

00:03:08.599 --> 00:03:10.399
- Plenty of non-compliant diabetics don't.

00:03:10.399 --> 00:03:13.069
- Wait, non-compliant
diabetics do get treated.

00:03:13.069 --> 00:03:14.209
I don't know why he said that.

00:03:14.209 --> 00:03:17.039
And patients who are addicted to food,

00:03:17.039 --> 00:03:19.959
or perhaps have an emotional issue,

00:03:19.959 --> 00:03:23.229
that is causing them to
overeat and become 600 pounds,

00:03:23.229 --> 00:03:24.879
or perhaps have a medical condition

00:03:24.879 --> 00:03:26.659
that's causing them to
gain this much weight,

00:03:26.659 --> 00:03:27.809
shouldn't be judged.

00:03:27.809 --> 00:03:31.119
Like you can't be a
doctor and be this judgy

00:03:31.119 --> 00:03:33.109
and be successful as a clinician.

00:03:33.109 --> 00:03:34.709
- We should do an LP and
look for neurosyphilis.

00:03:34.709 --> 00:03:36.169
- Is not syphilis.

00:03:36.169 --> 00:03:37.002
- How do you know?

00:03:37.002 --> 00:03:38.739
- Whatever he has is connected to his gut,

00:03:38.739 --> 00:03:39.619
not what's below it.

00:03:39.619 --> 00:03:41.279
MRI his brain, look for clots.

00:03:41.279 --> 00:03:42.899
(chiming sound)
- She said that the blood

00:03:42.899 --> 00:03:44.979
was drawn three times.
(whooshing sound)

00:03:44.979 --> 00:03:47.577
In that time they couldn't
check for syphilis?

00:03:47.577 --> 00:03:48.410
(booming sound)

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:49.862
- 1, 2, 3.

00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:54.862
(grunting)
(thudding noise)

00:03:56.049 --> 00:03:57.869
- Not going to lie this
is a true challenge

00:03:57.869 --> 00:03:59.809
for hospital and hospital systems,

00:03:59.809 --> 00:04:01.649
when patients can fit in the equipment.

00:04:01.649 --> 00:04:03.029
That's why we are trying to create

00:04:03.029 --> 00:04:05.670
newer equipment that is more capable

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:08.654
in handling patients of all
different sizes and shapes.

00:04:08.654 --> 00:04:11.239
- No bleeds, clots, infarcts.

00:04:11.239 --> 00:04:12.769
- I don't see any edema either.

00:04:12.769 --> 00:04:14.105
- So what do we do now?

00:04:14.105 --> 00:04:16.739
- An LP, even if it's not an STD,

00:04:16.739 --> 00:04:18.479
the fever points towards
some sort of infection.

00:04:18.479 --> 00:04:21.959
- [Chase] I'm not sure you
can do an LP on guy his size.

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:23.439
- Did they get a chest X-ray?

00:04:23.439 --> 00:04:25.349
Like, if you at least had a chest x-ray

00:04:25.349 --> 00:04:26.182
you could do that portable,

00:04:26.182 --> 00:04:27.139
you could do it right in the bed.

00:04:27.139 --> 00:04:29.009
You could see if that
there is a lung infection.

00:04:29.009 --> 00:04:29.909
You could rule that out.

00:04:29.909 --> 00:04:31.129
Check the urine, see if there's

00:04:31.129 --> 00:04:33.389
a urinary tract infection
with a pyelonephritis

00:04:33.389 --> 00:04:34.369
going up to the kidneys.

00:04:34.369 --> 00:04:37.199
Like why aren't they talking
about the most common things?

00:04:37.199 --> 00:04:39.869
This show is all about zebras, not horses.

00:04:39.869 --> 00:04:40.836
Remember that saying?
(horse neigh)

00:04:40.836 --> 00:04:43.199
"When you hear hoof beats,
think horses, not zebras."

00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:44.589
Doesn't mean you forget the zebras,

00:04:46.026 --> 00:04:46.859
(shouting)

00:04:46.859 --> 00:04:47.692
- Calm down man, calm down.

00:04:47.692 --> 00:04:48.525
(shouting)

00:04:48.525 --> 00:04:49.358
- Get him out already.

00:04:49.358 --> 00:04:50.305
- I'm trying.

00:04:50.305 --> 00:04:51.138
- Come on.

00:04:51.138 --> 00:04:53.388
(crashing)

00:04:55.298 --> 00:04:57.594
(panting)

00:04:57.594 --> 00:04:58.774
- We still have no idea why he was

00:04:58.774 --> 00:04:59.934
in the coma to begin with.

00:04:59.934 --> 00:05:02.564
- Or why he woke up. I
wonder if it is hormonal.

00:05:02.564 --> 00:05:05.244
Acute adrenal insufficiency
could cause a temporary coma.

00:05:05.244 --> 00:05:09.024
- It can, and also a thyroid
storm can cause a coma.

00:05:09.024 --> 00:05:11.284
Although a thyroid storm
would probably come

00:05:11.284 --> 00:05:14.494
with a rapid heart rate,
also not weight gain.

00:05:14.494 --> 00:05:16.150
If you have a hyperactive thyroid,

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:18.584
although if you have
an underactive thyroid

00:05:18.584 --> 00:05:20.564
followed by thyroid
storm, which can happen,

00:05:20.564 --> 00:05:22.804
cause the thyroid, even in like,

00:05:22.804 --> 00:05:25.914
certain conditions like
Hashimoto's and Graves' disease.

00:05:25.914 --> 00:05:27.444
Sometimes it flips on and off

00:05:27.444 --> 00:05:29.194
between hyper and hypothyroid.

00:05:29.194 --> 00:05:31.144
It's not always so clearly defined,

00:05:31.144 --> 00:05:34.234
especially in the beginning
of the onset of disease

00:05:34.234 --> 00:05:36.144
to later stages of disease.

00:05:36.144 --> 00:05:37.464
- Let's do a full STD panel

00:05:37.464 --> 00:05:38.994
and check his genitals for chancres.

00:05:38.994 --> 00:05:40.544
- Well, first of all, you
should have checked him

00:05:40.544 --> 00:05:42.644
for chancres beforehand
and you should have done

00:05:42.644 --> 00:05:44.904
a full STD panel before hand.

00:05:44.904 --> 00:05:47.194
It's like the cheapest test you can do.

00:05:47.194 --> 00:05:49.214
If you're talking about all these MRIs

00:05:49.214 --> 00:05:51.294
that you're breaking,
costing millions of dollars,

00:05:51.294 --> 00:05:53.774
the $300 STD panel would
have been worth it,

00:05:53.774 --> 00:05:54.884
Don't you think?

00:05:54.884 --> 00:05:57.014
- You having any problems
with your vision?

00:05:57.014 --> 00:06:00.032
- No, I have nystagmus. I've
had it since birth, I'm fine.

00:06:00.032 --> 00:06:02.234
- [Cameron] You're not fine,
you want a coma for two days.

00:06:02.234 --> 00:06:03.424
There's something wrong with you.

00:06:03.424 --> 00:06:05.644
- Was something wrong
with me, now I'm better.

00:06:05.644 --> 00:06:07.334
Now I'd like to go home.

00:06:07.334 --> 00:06:09.244
- The patient can always leave.

00:06:09.244 --> 00:06:12.321
Like, if he can think
about the consequences,

00:06:12.321 --> 00:06:15.484
if the patient can
verbalize those consequences

00:06:15.484 --> 00:06:17.534
and it's safe for them
to leave in that sense,

00:06:17.534 --> 00:06:18.564
they can choose to leave.

00:06:18.564 --> 00:06:21.704
They will leave against medical
advice, which is an AMA,

00:06:21.704 --> 00:06:23.834
but it's within their rights,
it's patient autonomy.

00:06:23.834 --> 00:06:25.384
- Wait, let us take you
all the way outside.

00:06:25.384 --> 00:06:26.627
- I'm fine.

00:06:26.627 --> 00:06:29.274
- It doesn't matter, it's hospital rules.

00:06:29.274 --> 00:06:31.939
- Oh, screw the rules, I've
been on my back for four days.

00:06:31.939 --> 00:06:33.744
I need the exercise, right?

00:06:33.744 --> 00:06:35.664
- George, come on, let us
just take you to the taxi.

00:06:35.664 --> 00:06:37.384
- Don't worry, I may not be able

00:06:37.384 --> 00:06:39.498
to climb Everest, but I can walk.

00:06:39.498 --> 00:06:42.248
(suspense music)

00:06:45.935 --> 00:06:46.768
(dinging sound)
- First of all,

00:06:46.768 --> 00:06:49.164
if a patient has not gotten up

00:06:49.164 --> 00:06:51.004
since he's been admitted to the hospital,

00:06:51.004 --> 00:06:53.257
would get a PT evaluation to see

00:06:53.257 --> 00:06:55.404
if he's capable of walking on his own.

00:06:55.404 --> 00:06:58.084
And if he is not, he can
not safely go home alone.

00:06:58.084 --> 00:07:00.694
He would at least need to
go to a subacute rehab stay

00:07:00.694 --> 00:07:02.684
where he could learn
how to walk on his own

00:07:02.684 --> 00:07:03.604
and take care of himself.

00:07:03.604 --> 00:07:05.264
'Cause if a patient can't get up and walk,

00:07:05.264 --> 00:07:06.824
you're essentially setting
them up for failure

00:07:06.824 --> 00:07:07.999
by sending them home.

00:07:07.999 --> 00:07:08.971
- George?

00:07:08.971 --> 00:07:12.150
(glass smashes)

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:13.961
- I think we should focus
on the coma and the fever.

00:07:13.961 --> 00:07:15.474
- We have a rather large piece

00:07:15.474 --> 00:07:17.364
of tempered glass that begs to differ.

00:07:17.364 --> 00:07:19.894
- I just mean it's not connected.

00:07:19.894 --> 00:07:21.214
- How could you possibly know?

00:07:21.214 --> 00:07:22.164
- Because I did it.

00:07:23.594 --> 00:07:25.034
I didn't think he should be discharged,

00:07:25.034 --> 00:07:27.852
so I gave him three grams of phenytoin.

00:07:27.852 --> 00:07:29.329
(spitting noise)

00:07:29.329 --> 00:07:30.794
- What hospital is this?

00:07:30.794 --> 00:07:33.434
She drugged the patient
so he wouldn't leave.

00:07:33.434 --> 00:07:34.865
Oh my God.

00:07:34.865 --> 00:07:36.440
(clanking noise)

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:37.273
(comical music playing)

00:07:37.273 --> 00:07:38.884
Don't poison your patients.

00:07:38.884 --> 00:07:40.294
Even if you think it's right for them

00:07:40.294 --> 00:07:41.621
because you are not God.

00:07:41.621 --> 00:07:42.534
(screen whooshes)

00:07:42.534 --> 00:07:44.954
- [Sam] What would happen to her...?

00:07:44.954 --> 00:07:46.504
- Medical license loss?

00:07:46.504 --> 00:07:47.337
We'll do it in order.

00:07:47.337 --> 00:07:50.044
They find out, medical review committee,

00:07:50.044 --> 00:07:51.684
medical board suspension,

00:07:51.684 --> 00:07:53.724
followed by medical board reevaluation,

00:07:53.724 --> 00:07:55.374
followed by loss of medical license,

00:07:55.374 --> 00:07:56.964
either temporarily or forever,

00:07:56.964 --> 00:07:59.974
depending on how angry the patient is,

00:07:59.974 --> 00:08:03.104
prior situations, et cetera, et cetera.

00:08:03.104 --> 00:08:03.937
Lawyer up.

00:08:03.937 --> 00:08:06.124
- It can't be Chagas, he's
never been outside the country.

00:08:06.124 --> 00:08:07.264
- But his stomach has.

00:08:07.264 --> 00:08:09.864
The food we eat no longer
comes from America's heartland.

00:08:09.864 --> 00:08:12.644
It comes from South America's
deforested jungle land

00:08:12.644 --> 00:08:15.284
where lettuce now grows and
lettuce pickers now poop.

00:08:15.284 --> 00:08:16.574
Get a sample of his CSF.

00:08:16.574 --> 00:08:17.624
- How are you going to get a sample

00:08:17.624 --> 00:08:18.914
of his cerebral spinal fluid?

00:08:18.914 --> 00:08:19.747
That's going to be tough.

00:08:19.747 --> 00:08:22.634
- [George] You want to
drill a hole in my head?

00:08:22.634 --> 00:08:25.643
It's gotta be something
other than a parasite.

00:08:25.643 --> 00:08:28.864
I buy my produce at the
best market in town,

00:08:28.864 --> 00:08:30.324
and I always wash it.

00:08:30.324 --> 00:08:32.124
- [Cameron] Leafy vegetables
can suck contaminated water

00:08:32.124 --> 00:08:33.644
through the roots right into the plant.

00:08:33.644 --> 00:08:36.534
- I've never heard this,
this sounds farfetched,

00:08:36.534 --> 00:08:38.967
but I hope they researched this.

00:08:38.967 --> 00:08:41.717
(drilling noise)

00:08:42.774 --> 00:08:45.454
Please tell me this is a
neurosurgeon doing this,

00:08:45.454 --> 00:08:46.667
Not like, Foreman.

00:08:48.374 --> 00:08:50.144
It's Foreman, oh my God.

00:08:50.144 --> 00:08:52.034
These are they're all neurosurgeons,

00:08:52.034 --> 00:08:55.474
infectious disease specialists,
family medicine doctors,

00:08:55.474 --> 00:08:58.504
cardiologists, they run MRI machines...

00:08:58.504 --> 00:09:00.364
Oh cramp!

00:09:00.364 --> 00:09:01.664
- What'd you do?

00:09:01.664 --> 00:09:02.937
- Nothing, why? What's wrong?

00:09:02.937 --> 00:09:05.014
- I can't, I can't see.

00:09:05.014 --> 00:09:06.404
- Vision's blurry or you've lost it?

00:09:06.404 --> 00:09:08.394
- I didn't lose it, you took it from me.

00:09:08.394 --> 00:09:09.227
- [Cameron] George, calm down.

00:09:09.227 --> 00:09:10.234
- I can't see!

00:09:10.234 --> 00:09:11.154
- This is actually why patients are

00:09:11.154 --> 00:09:13.104
kept awake during some neuro procedures,

00:09:13.104 --> 00:09:15.144
because in case you do touch something,

00:09:15.144 --> 00:09:16.294
you want to know right away

00:09:16.294 --> 00:09:18.374
so that you don't continue
doing damage to that area.

00:09:18.374 --> 00:09:20.175
- Blindness plus comma says diabetes.

00:09:20.175 --> 00:09:22.174
- No, blood sugar, urine dipstick,

00:09:22.174 --> 00:09:23.644
and hemoglobin A1-C are normal.

00:09:23.644 --> 00:09:25.334
- Were normal, when you tested.

00:09:25.334 --> 00:09:26.414
He's been in and out of a coma,

00:09:26.414 --> 00:09:28.294
Whatever's going on is waxing and waning.

00:09:28.294 --> 00:09:30.114
- That's not true because
the hemoglobin A1-C

00:09:30.114 --> 00:09:31.724
gives you an average of the blood sugars

00:09:31.724 --> 00:09:32.904
over the last three months.

00:09:32.904 --> 00:09:34.614
So it doesn't mean it was normal today,

00:09:34.614 --> 00:09:36.234
and it could be different tomorrow.

00:09:36.234 --> 00:09:38.504
It still will give you the
average of the last three months.

00:09:38.504 --> 00:09:39.717
So I don't agree with House here.

00:09:39.717 --> 00:09:41.844
- I'm not diabetic.

00:09:41.844 --> 00:09:43.084
- As soon as you drink
this, the sooner I get

00:09:43.084 --> 00:09:45.514
to go waste my time with something else.

00:09:45.514 --> 00:09:46.524
- Get off of me.

00:09:46.524 --> 00:09:48.181
- No dessert till you
finished your dinner.

00:09:48.181 --> 00:09:50.444
(struggling sounds)

00:09:50.444 --> 00:09:51.594
- It's a classic House situation,

00:09:51.594 --> 00:09:53.674
where he's just losing his
mind, fighting a patient.

00:09:53.674 --> 00:09:56.354
(shouting)

00:09:56.354 --> 00:09:57.187
- Go.

00:09:58.106 --> 00:10:00.084
- Oh wait, his liver is failing now?

00:10:00.084 --> 00:10:01.474
He's jaundiced? What's happening?

00:10:01.474 --> 00:10:03.024
- Get x-rays of his hands.

00:10:03.024 --> 00:10:05.605
Tell him the lung cancer is in
no way connected to obesity.

00:10:05.605 --> 00:10:08.014
- You don't think he'll
realize we're lying?

00:10:08.014 --> 00:10:09.727
- You're not, lung cancer
is got nothing to do with...

00:10:09.727 --> 00:10:12.294
- I meant about him having lung cancer.

00:10:12.294 --> 00:10:13.894
- You didn't notice his fingers?

00:10:14.734 --> 00:10:15.654
- I noticed they were fat.

00:10:15.654 --> 00:10:17.354
- You should've pissed him off.

00:10:17.354 --> 00:10:19.544
He would have grabbed you and
you would have felt the bones.

00:10:19.544 --> 00:10:21.727
They're not just fat, they're clubbed.

00:10:23.474 --> 00:10:26.764
- This is the first x-ray
they're getting? Are you joking?

00:10:26.764 --> 00:10:28.744
He was in a coma in the ICU

00:10:28.744 --> 00:10:30.807
and they're just getting an x-ray now?

00:10:32.274 --> 00:10:34.654
Oh, they're getting an x-ray of his hand.

00:10:34.654 --> 00:10:36.634
I thought there's the first
chest x-ray they're getting.

00:10:36.634 --> 00:10:38.834
- Your tests were positive.

00:10:39.674 --> 00:10:42.932
You have a small cell lung carcinoma.

00:10:42.932 --> 00:10:45.474
It caused a paraneoplastic
neurologic syndrome.

00:10:45.474 --> 00:10:47.384
- Paraneoplastic basically means that

00:10:47.384 --> 00:10:50.804
as a result of having this
tumor, it's secreted hormones

00:10:50.804 --> 00:10:54.204
that have altered the way
that his body behaves.

00:10:54.204 --> 00:10:56.114
- It's inoperable, but there there are

00:10:56.114 --> 00:10:57.416
radiation treatments available.

00:10:57.416 --> 00:11:00.166
- (soft gasping)

00:11:01.234 --> 00:11:03.824
- They might give you a few more months.

00:11:03.824 --> 00:11:04.884
- I never smoked.

00:11:04.884 --> 00:11:07.434
- Not all lung cancer cases
are caused by smoking.

00:11:07.434 --> 00:11:08.977
Some are caused by asbestos.

00:11:08.977 --> 00:11:12.279
(footsteps walking away)

00:11:12.279 --> 00:11:15.454
(solemn music playing)

00:11:15.454 --> 00:11:17.644
I would ask if he had any
questions at that point.

00:11:17.644 --> 00:11:20.154
I mean, you're just delivering
a life-changing diagnosis,

00:11:20.154 --> 00:11:21.964
you spend 10 seconds and you leave.

00:11:21.964 --> 00:11:23.694
I think that's kind of cold.

00:11:23.694 --> 00:11:27.334
- Check out the harsh reality
of being 800 plus pounds here.

00:11:27.334 --> 00:11:30.423
Click it, check it out, as
always stay happy and healthy.

00:11:30.423 --> 00:11:33.506
(upbeat outro music)

